# Git & no-revert safety (mandatory)

**Git: `git commit` ONLY.** You may run `git commit` and read-only inspection
(`git status` / `git log` / `git diff`). Do NOT run any other Git operation —
no `checkout`, `switch`, `reset`, `revert`, `restore`, `clean`, `rm`, `mv`,
`stash`, `rebase`, `merge`, `cherry-pick`, `branch -D`, `push`, `pull`,
`fetch --prune`, or any `--force`. Record renames/removals with normal file
tools and let the next `git commit` capture them — never `git rm`/`git mv`/`git reset`.
Never push/sync to a remote unless explicitly asked.

**Never revert code.** Do NOT revert, undo, roll back, overwrite, discard, or
delete existing code, files, or the user's local (uncommitted) changes. Only move
**forward** — add, extend, or fix in place; change the minimum needed. If a change
looks wrong, surface it and ask — do not silently revert. Treat the working tree
as authoritative; never replace it with an older snapshot or a fresh clone.

